;;; A string written in a rule's `:match` never matches — M1e2 **W18**,
;;; register row `W18/str-in-match` (`corpus/wrong_answers.toml`), banked by
;;; S1e2.2.18 (2026-11-04) from S1e2.2.16's parked note, where it was
;;; `build/s1e2.2.16/textkey/str-in-match.ein`.
;;;
;;; ── What this program is ───────────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; Four `holds` facts: the int `1`, the string `"1"`, the atom `a` and the
;;; string `"b"`. The loader holds a string written as a fact's argument as
;;; the symbol it names (`01_grammar.md`'s `STRING` row), so `(holds "b")` is
;;; the fact `(holds b)`, and `(holds "1")` holds the symbol `1`, a value
;;; apart from the int. Three rules match one `holds` fact each: `s` writes
;;; the string `"1"`, `t` the atom `b`, `u` the int `1`. `t` and `u` are the
;;; controls.
;;;
;;; ── The answer, by hand ────────────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; A string in a rule means what it means in a fact, so `s` matches
;;; `(holds "1")` as `t` matches `(holds "b")`: root derives `(hit s)`,
;;; `(hit t)` and `(hit u)`. The blind enumerator proposes `(hit 1)`,
;;; `(hit a)` and `(hit b)`, nothing refutes, so R is their eight subsets,
;;; and every state holds the three `hit` facts root derives.
;;;
;;; ── What the engine answered, and why it does not any more ─────────────
;;;
;;; Until S1e2.2.18 `t` and `u` fired and `s` never did: the rule compiler
;;; did not lower a string, so in a `:match` it was an opaque slot that
;;; matched nothing (`compile.rs`' `slot`). R was the eight subsets, and no
;;; state held `(hit s)`: `ein solve FILE --minimal` at `ff023413` printed
;;; root's state as `(model (hit t) (hit u))`, the summary's
;;; `root.facts_by_relation.hit` 2, in a release build and a debug one.
;;; Since then the compiler lowers a string as the loader does, the symbol it
;;; names, and root's state is `(model (hit s) (hit t) (hit u))`.

(relation holds Node) (relation hit Node)
(holds 1) (holds "1") (holds a) (holds "b")
(rule s () :match (holds "1") :assert (hit s))
(rule t () :match (holds b) :assert (hit t))
(rule u () :match (holds 1) :assert (hit u))
